            ECHO OF THE FIRST FEW INPUT CARDS -
 INPUT CARD>! C2H7O3Cl | INTERMEDIATE BASIS TEST -- def2-SVP                                
 INPUT CARD>!                                                                               
 INPUT CARD>! ============================================================                  
 INPUT CARD>! Why this file exists                                                          
 INPUT CARD>! ============================================================                  
 INPUT CARD>! caa005bIRCCML1st-prerelax-631Gdp.inp confirmed a genuine minimum at           
 INPUT CARD>! wB97X-D/6-31G(d,p)/PCM(water): EXECUTION TERMINATED NORMALLY, and the         
 INPUT CARD>! final Hessian's vibrational analysis showed ZERO IMAGINARY FREQUENCIES.       
 INPUT CARD>!                                                                               
 INPUT CARD>! caa005bIRCCML1stdef2TZVPPe.inp then took that CONFIRMED-GOOD geometry and     
 INPUT CARD>! simply switched the basis to def2-TZVPP (same functional, same PCM            
 INPUT CARD>! treatment, same geometry) -- and immediately showed 16 SIMULTANEOUS           
 INPUT CARD>! NEGATIVE EIGENVALUES, reproducing closely across two independently            
 INPUT CARD>! computed Hessians at NSERCH=2 and NSERCH=3. This ruled out the                
 INPUT CARD>! "wrong level of theory generated this geometry" explanation: the geometry     
 INPUT CARD>! is now confirmed correct for this functional/solvent combination, and the     
 INPUT CARD>! instability appeared purely from enlarging the basis set.                     
 INPUT CARD>!                                                                               
 INPUT CARD>! Leading hypothesis: basis-set superposition / basis-flexibility effects.      
 INPUT CARD>! def2-TZVPP is a much larger, more diffuse, more polarized basis than          
 INPUT CARD>! 6-31G(d,p), and larger/more diffuse basis sets are well known to cause        
 INPUT CARD>! exactly this kind of problem specifically for WEAKLY-BOUND, MULTI-FRAGMENT    
 INPUT CARD>! complexes (this is 3 separate molecules held together only by H-bonds):       
 INPUT CARD>! basis functions centred on one fragment can "borrow" density from a           
 INPUT CARD>! neighbouring fragment once the basis is flexible/diffuse enough, creating     
 INPUT CARD>! spurious extra soft/unstable directions that are an artefact of basis         
 INPUT CARD>! completeness rather than real chemistry.                                      
 INPUT CARD>!                                                                               
 INPUT CARD>! ============================================================                  
 INPUT CARD>! What this file does                                                           
 INPUT CARD>! ============================================================                  
 INPUT CARD>! Tests the middle ground: def2-SVP (same Karlsruhe/def2 family as the          
 INPUT CARD>! final target def2-TZVPP, but much smaller/less diffuse -- roughly             
 INPUT CARD>! comparable in size to 6-31G(d,p)) at the SAME confirmed-good geometry.        
 INPUT CARD>!                                                                               
 INPUT CARD>! If def2-SVP behaves cleanly (few/no negative eigenvalues, similar to          
 INPUT CARD>! 6-31G(d,p)): supports the basis-completeness/diffuseness hypothesis, and      
 INPUT CARD>! the practical path forward is a staged basis escalation (SVP -> TZVPP)        
 INPUT CARD>! rather than jumping straight to the largest basis.                            
 INPUT CARD>!                                                                               
 INPUT CARD>! If def2-SVP ALSO shows many negative eigenvalues despite being a small        
 INPUT CARD>! basis: the instability is tied to the def2 family's specific functions        
 INPUT CARD>! (contraction/polarization scheme) rather than sheer size/diffuseness --       
 INPUT CARD>! a different and more surprising finding, worth stopping to think about        
 INPUT CARD>! (and a good candidate to post to the GAMESS Google Group either way).         
 INPUT CARD>!                                                                               
 INPUT CARD>! Optimizer settings unchanged from TZVPPd/e (conservative DXMAX=0.01,          
 INPUT CARD>! IHREP=2, METHOD=SEMINUM, Cartesian coordinates, NOSYM=1, DIRSCF=.TRUE.)       
 INPUT CARD>! since these reflect facts about wB97X-D itself (no analytic Hessian),         
 INPUT CARD>! not about basis size.                                                         
